  subject-identity probe (un-normalised)       0.121  (chance 0.018)
     Features identify WHO at 6.9x chance, while identifying WHICH HAND at only 0.536.
     Per-subject z-scoring is what removes this -- it is doing its job.

  un-normalised: naive random split 0.544 vs correct LOSO 0.534  ->  inflation +0.010

  z-scored: naive random split 0.548 vs correct LOSO 0.532  ->  inflation +0.015

     Note: the usual random-split inflation does NOT appear here, and the
     reason is worth stating. That inflation comes from subject identity
     carrying label information. Every subject here is ~50/50 left/right,
     so knowing WHO a trial came from says nothing about WHICH HAND, and
     leaking subject identity buys the model nothing. The dangerous leak in
     this dataset is temporal (the previous trial), not per-subject.

